   "t": 61.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/1Bpp4/3P1B2/4PN2/PPP2PPP/RN1QK2R b KQkq - 3 5",
   "line": [
    "Bb5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop to b5 is a little odd — in the London you usually play c3 first. Black could play the natural bishop to d7, but many of you see the queen to a5 with check. It does not win the bishop, because of the knight to c3 — but at this level you play moves not to win material, but to induce an undesirable one."
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 131.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/qBpp4/3P1B2/4PN2/PPP2PPP/RN1QK2R w KQkq - 4 6",
   "line": [
    "Qa5+"
   ],
   "spoken": "The whole point of the London is that the pawn on d4 is a stronghold — mega-defended once White plays c3. So the queen to a5 with check induces the knight to c3, preventing that c3, and d4 turns from a strength into a weakness."
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 142.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/qBpp4/3P1B2/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 5 6",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 166.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/qBpp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 6 7",
   "line": [
    "Bg4"
   ],
   "spoken": "After the knight to c3, play actively — not e6, which is underdeveloped and allows the knight to e5. Restrict the knight on f3 while developing: the bishop to g4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 172.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp2pppp/2B2n2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "Bxc6+"
   ],
   "spoken": "It pressures the knight that defends d4. White takes, Black recaptures — a compact, pleasant pawn structure."
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 173.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p3pppp/2p2n2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 8",
